The [[Hoggaanka system]] was a standardized system of governance established by [[Marcel II]] of the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] in 1105. It broadly set obligations of cliques and families that had ruled portions of Burawa as part of the [[Family rule system (Burawa)|Family rule system]] and codified them into laws that helped manage Burawa's vast kingdom.

== Rules ==
The Hoggaanka system gives the King of Burawa the right to create a [[Hogan]], where he could assign a [[Hoga]] to rule with his family indefinitely, the Hoga would be obligated to defend Burawa in times of conflict and provide men and resources in exchange from patronage from the king's central territories. The Hoga could be removed by the King, but it would requir approval from the [[Burawi Catholic Church]] as well. The Hoga could rule his territory as he wishes with no interference from the Kingdom. The Hoga was allowed to expand it's territory in lands not ruled by the kingdom or other Hogas.

== History ==
=== Classic Hoggaanka System ===
The establishment of the Hoggaanka system arose from a governance crisis in 1103, where the ruling family of [[Waxo Waga]] had no heirs and the land had been contested by the family that had run [[Laasqoray#Middle Ages|Laasqoray]], the conflict had gained notice of the king, who had discovered the wide informal system that was established by his predecessor [[Iacob II]] in 945 after the [[Northern Conquest of Burawa]]. The king had established a set of reforms to formalize this system. 

The Hoggaanka created a secondary government along Burawa's borders with unique societies emerging in a far flung region of Burawa. The Hoggaanka had carried into the Empire of Burawa, where it had assisted in expanding it's territory rapidly in the 1400's. The Hoga system eventually became more weak, as the central Kingdom became more wealthy and borders on the mainland more established. 

=== Hoga Senate ===
The Hogas had remained relatively powerless beyond administrators until the [[Burawa Imperial Crisis]] of 1706, where the empire had split into two, the Hogas that remained loyal to the [[Eastern Burawi Empire]] had forced the new King, [[Iacob IV]] to create a representation system for the lords known as the [[Hoga Senate]].

The Hoga Senate was a political house that represented all 114 Hogan in Burawa. The system allowed the ability for Hoga to represent themselves to the King's court, and provide a system for introducing bills to the King. While the King had absolute domain over the the non-Hogan Burawa, the Hogan would now only be regulated by this body with the King representing 57 votes, or a third of the total vote share in the senate.

The Hoga Senate was merged into the [[Parliament of the Kingdom of Burawa]] in 1738 after the [[Merchant's Revolution]] where an establishment of a people's representative government was demanded. The Hoga Senate had been renamed to the [[House of Governors (Burawa)|House of Governors]] as now it contained representatives both from Hogans and those from Governors that managed [[Centralized Burawa]]. The merger was seen as a way to dilute power from the Hoga, as the King had the ultimate ability to nominate governors, who then appointed representatives to the house.